    "last_assistant_text": "Still no crash. Let me think about what might be going wrong. The jq program `. == .` applied to input Nan4000 should compare the NaN with itself. But does `.` reference the input? And will `jv_equal` be called (not `jv_cmp`)?\n\nWait, I need to think about the fuzz target flow more carefully:\n\n```cpp\nif (jq_compile(jq, prog_payload.c_str())) {\n    jv input = jv_parse(parse_payload1.c_str());\n    if (jv_is_valid(input)) {\n        jq_start(jq, input, 0);\n        jv next = jv_parse(parse_payload2.c_str());\n        if (jv_is_valid(next)) {\n            jv actual = jq_next(jq);\n            jv_free(actual);\n        }\n        jv_free(next);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe `jq_next(jq)` executes the program on the input. But `next` (parse_payload2) isn't passed to jq_next at all — it's just parsed and checked. The actual execution is `jq_next` which runs the compiled program with the input set by `jq_start`.\n\nSo the program `. == .` should:\n1. Duplicate `.` (the input, which is NaN4000)\n2. Compare them with `==`\n3. This calls `binop_equal` → `jv_equal` → `jvp_number_equal` → `jvp_number_cmp` → `decNumberCompare`\n\nBut `jv_is_valid(next)` needs to be true. `jv_parse(\"0\")` should be valid. \n\nHmm but maybe the issue is that the compiled harness doesn't actually use `<fuzzer/FuzzedDataProvider.h>` the way I think. Let me check if maybe the harness is compiled differently or has a different binary. Let me try a simpler approach — just feed the input that the fix test uses: `1 != .` with `Nan4000`.\n\nActually wait. Let me re-examine the FuzzedDataProvider implementation more carefully. The version included in the compiler might differ from what I found. Some versions use a different algorithm. Let me look for another version.",
